emails.content:
Hi Mara,

I’ve now heard back from Northway Freight regarding the claim for the pallet of gauge housings and telemetry batteries that was due at the Calder depot on 14 March but did not arrive until 22 March.

They have accepted that the eight-day delay fell outside the three-day service in consignment note NF-784193. They have also agreed that the pallet was incorrectly held at the Preston hub from 15 to 20 March, rather than being transferred to the Calder route as their tracking updates originally suggested.

Their revised offer is £1,486.70, broken down as follows:

• £612.00 refund of the priority freight charge;
• £524.70 for the replacement hire of the temporary telemetry units;
• £350.00 as a contribution towards the additional technician visit on 19 March.

This is an improvement on the £962.00 offer they sent on 9 April, but it is still £438.30 below the £1,925.00 total in our claim schedule dated 2 April. They have again declined the remaining call-out cost, saying the second vehicle and overtime were an operational choice rather than a direct consequence of the delay.

I pushed back on that point because your incident note, “Calder Gauge Deployment Delay – 19 March,” clearly explains why two technicians were required to keep the temporary sensor operating while the damaged battery enclosure was made safe. I also reminded them that the original delivery booking listed the equipment as time-critical for flood-monitoring continuity. They have asked for one final item before reconsidering: a copy of the duty rota or job allocation showing that the 19 March visit was additional to the planned maintenance schedule.

Could you send me either the relevant page from the March field rota or an email from Tom confirming the extra attendance? Please redact personal mobile numbers if they appear. If I can forward that by 4 p.m. on Friday, 26 April, Northway have said their claims manager will issue a final decision by 3 May.

For reference, I now have the following documents in the claim file:

• consignment note NF-784193;
• Northway tracking history, 13–22 March;
• delivery receipt signed at 10:42 on 22 March;
• supplier invoice RW-11847 for £8,760.00;
• telemetry hire invoice HST-4408 for £524.70;
• your incident note dated 20 March;
• claim schedule dated 2 April; and
• Northway’s revised offer letter dated 23 April.

Unless you think otherwise, I recommend that we do not accept the £1,486.70 yet. The offer remains open until 8 May, so there is enough time to provide the rota evidence and pursue the outstanding £438.30 without risking the amount already conceded. Northway have confirmed in writing that supplying further evidence will not withdraw the current offer.

Also, their letter refers to the contents as “general electrical equipment.” I’ll correct that in my response to “river-gauge telemetry equipment and sealed battery housings,” as described on the booking form. That distinction may help reinforce why the delivery date mattered.

Thanks again for tracking down the temporary-unit paperwork last week. Send the rota extract when you have a moment, and I’ll submit the complete response under claim reference CLM-55927.

Best,

Leanne Whitaker

Hi Priya,

Thanks for forwarding Elena’s confirmation. I’ve added the viewing for Saturday 14 June at 10:30 a.m. to my calendar and saved the brochure and the draft tenancy agreement she attached. I’ll meet you outside 18 Calder Wharf at 10:20 rather than at the station, as agreed. The entrance is apparently beside the Waterside Pharmacy, not through the courtyard shown on the listing.

I’ve gone back through our notes and made a final checklist so we don’t forget anything while we’re there. The advertised rent is £1,425 per month, with a £1,644.23 deposit, and Elena said the holding deposit would be £328.85 if we decide to apply. She also confirmed that the rent includes the service charge but not council tax, electricity, water or broadband. The flat is in council-tax band C; I’d still like to ask what the current tenants actually pay rather than rely on the estimate in the brochure.

The main things I want to check are:

• whether there has been any damp around the bedroom window or the patched section behind the sofa;
• the water pressure in the shower and kitchen tap;
• whether the washing machine and fridge shown in the photographs are included;
• the exact dimensions of the smaller bedroom, since the floor plan in “Calder Wharf particulars.pdf” says 2.7 m by 2.4 m but Elena’s message said 2.6 m by 2.3 m;
• whether bicycles can be kept in the locked ground-floor store;
• how audible the lift and riverside delivery entrance are from the bedrooms;
• whether the proposed 12-month tenancy includes the six-month break clause shown in clause 9.2 of “Draft AST – Flat 7.pdf”.

I’ll also take a few photos of the window seals and the river-facing wall, provided Elena is happy with that. Given how close the building is to the Calder, I want to ask for the building’s flood-risk assessment and any history of water entering the basement or cycle store. The Environment Agency map shows the block outside the higher-risk outline, but local drainage can be a different story. I promise not to turn the viewing into a gauge-station inspection!

For paperwork, I’ll bring my passport, three latest payslips and the employment letter from the regional water authority. You said you have your passport and bank statements, so between us we should be ready if we want to submit the “Tenant Application Form v3” that afternoon. I have not transferred any money; as we agreed, neither of us will pay the holding deposit until we have seen the flat, checked Elena’s agency credentials and received the payment details on the agency’s headed document.

One small timing issue: I’m running the rescue-team equipment check at 8:00 on Saturday. It should finish by 9:15, leaving plenty of time, but if an exercise overruns I’ll text you by 9:30. Please go ahead with the 10:30 viewing if I’m delayed—I can join by video for the second pass through the rooms. My phone will be in the dry bag during the river segment, so a missed call before 9:15 won’t mean anything is wrong.

If everything matches what we’ve been told, I’m still comfortable offering the listed £1,425 rather than bidding above it. My preference would be a tenancy beginning Monday 7 July, subject to references, the inventory and written confirmation of the break clause. Could you check that you’re still happy with that start date before Saturday?

See you at 10:20 by the pharmacy. I’ll bring a tape measure and the printed checklist.

Best,
Mara

Hi Priya,

Thanks for checking the renewal pack against the Harbour Authority’s list. I’ve now dealt with the two points you flagged in your email yesterday.

Dr Bennett completed the ENG1-equivalent medical assessment this morning, 18 September, and the signed certificate is attached as “Ellis_Medical_Certificate_18-09-2025.pdf”. It is valid until 17 September 2027. The surgery corrected the first version, which had my date of birth transposed, so please use only the file attached here and discard the scan I sent at 09:12.

I have also revised Form HP-12, “Harbour Pilot Certificate Renewal Application”. Section 4 now shows 46 qualifying pilotage movements between 1 October 2024 and 31 August 2025, rather than 44. The two omitted movements were the North Star inbound on 14 June and the Calder Trader outbound on 22 July. Both are recorded in the Harbour Master’s movement ledger, and Tomas Reed has countersigned the amended sea-time sheet. The updated documents are attached as “HP-12_Renewal_MEllis_v4.pdf” and “Pilotage_Record_Oct24-Aug25_signed.pdf”.

For clarity, the pack should now contain:

- completed and signed Form HP-12, version 4;
- current medical certificate dated 18 September 2025;
- signed pilotage record showing 46 movements;
- VHF/DSC certificate copy;
- first-aid certificate renewed on 6 April 2025;
- passport-style photograph taken on 2 September;
- copy of my existing Class B harbour pilot certificate, HPB-3174.

My current certificate expires on 30 September 2025, so I would be grateful if you could submit the pack by Friday, 19 September, as we discussed. That leaves the Authority seven working days before expiry. The renewal fee shown on the latest schedule is £285, not the £265 quoted on the February form. Finance confirmed this afternoon that purchase order RWA-PO-78431 has been increased to £285; the revised PO is attached. Please ask the certification office to invoice Regional Water Authority rather than charging the card details held from my 2022 renewal.

One point remains unclear: the guidance note says applicants must attend a local-knowledge interview if fewer than 50 movements have been completed during the qualifying year. However, Hannah Cole’s message of 11 September said the Harbour Master had approved an exemption because my gauge-maintenance call-outs account for the shortfall and I completed the chart-work assessment on 28 August with a score of 92%. Could you include Hannah’s email with the submission and ask for written confirmation that no interview is required? I do not want the application held as incomplete after the deadline.

I’ll be at the Lower Weir gauge tomorrow from 07:30 until roughly 13:00 and may not have reliable reception. If Certification Services need a quick clarification, text me and I’ll call back once I’m clear of the chamber. I’m available after 14:30 tomorrow or any time Monday morning.

Thanks again for keeping track of this—particularly the revised fee and the medical correction. Please let me know when the portal receipt and application reference come through.

Best,

Mara
